# ---------------------------------------------------------------- abstract
replace_between("## Abstract\n", "Keywords:", """## Abstract

Farm management information systems (FMIS) accumulate years of records that are seldom used for prediction, while the crop simulators that could use them need inputs a farmer does not have. This single-farm diagnostic study asks what the operational records of one commercial irrigated potato farm in southern Sweden can teach a simple, planning-grade crop model embedded in the open-source QGIS plugin GeoDataFarm, meaning a model intended for seasonal planning and scenario comparison rather than research-grade simulation, and how the same records behave in the plugin's within-field productivity index. Twenty-two potato field-years from 2015 to 2025, with 48 variety-level yield observations, were evaluated by leave-one-field-year-out cross-validation against literature defaults, a training-set farm mean and a training-set same-variety mean, with learning curves, bootstrap resampling of parameters and pre-specified sensitivity runs; the index was scored on 21 consecutive-year field pairs. Under the plugin's implemented default, which models an unlogged nutrient as zero applied, calibration reduced the typical error from 78 % to 49 % of the mean yield but left a 21 t/ha bias, because the unlogged potassium floor capped every estimate and drove potential yield to its search bound. In a post hoc diagnostic variant that treats an unlogged nutrient as unknown, the error fell from 58 % to 31 %, still above the farm mean's 27 %; the same-variety mean, at 22 %, was the strongest predictor evaluated, and refitting potential yield per variety only brought the model level with the farm mean. Potential yield was comparatively stable under resampling (90 % of bootstrap fits within 82 to 97 t/ha) whereas the nitrogen parameters spanned most of their ranges. The previous season's yield map ranked next-season yield with a median Spearman correlation of 0.18; the plugin's equal-weight combination with soil layers performed worse in 12 of 13 pairs, and a sign-aware weighting recovered approximately the yield-only level without demonstrating added value from the soil layers. In this dataset the operational data semantics, how a missing record is interpreted and how heterogeneous layers are combined, determined what the models could identify; the results are case-study evidence, not a general evaluation of crop models in potato.

""")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- introduction
