# new 2.8 after 2.7 (before Section 3)
rep("## 3. Results", """### 2.8 Data quality screening and reproducibility

Operational yield maps arrive with defects that a research dataset would not have, and the way they are screened changes the result. Two quality levels are therefore reported. All operational records uses every field-year the plugin's scan accepted. Quality-screened records additionally excludes field-years whose field-mean potato yield exceeds 80 t/ha, above the crop's attainable yield in Sweden and so a probable monitor calibration or unit error; three field-years met that rule. For the index, a harvest map enters only if it covers at least 300 grid cells and its field median is positive, and a map whose values mix kilograms and tonnes per hectare within one year, detected as values more than two orders of magnitude from the field median, is converted to the majority unit before ranking; eight maps were converted, in one case for 466 of 1285 cells. The plugin's own import already removes implausible points and corrects sensor time lag, but has no headland, start-up or coverage filter; those would belong in a fuller pipeline and were not applied here.

Two software defects were found during the study and their status is traceable (Table 5): the irrigation loader summed overlapping passes as if each covered the whole field, and the fertiliser loader discarded imported journal rows with a source-table reference. The irrigation fix is in the exported data analysed here; the fertiliser rates were merged from the journal dump by the analysis script because the defect was found after the export, and the fixed loader yields the same records. Both fixes carry regression tests. The exported, anonymised tables, the analysis scripts, the protocol with its change log and the plugin at the analysed commit are archived together, and one command regenerates every table and figure from the tables.

## 3. Results""")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Section 3 wholesale
replace_between("## 3. Results\n", "## 4. Discussion", """## 3. Results

### 3.1 Dataset and exclusions

The Hörte database held yield maps for 36 fields and 117 field-years in total (Table 2). Of these, 22 field-years produced a usable training example, all potato and spread over ten of the harvest years 2015 to 2025 with none in 2022, giving 48 variety observations once fields planted with several varieties were split by variety (Figure 2a). The dominant reason for exclusion was the absence of a planting record in the harvest year, which accounted for 50 field-years and corresponds almost entirely to the cereal and oilseed rape seasons of the rotation, for which the farm logged harvest but not sowing (Figure 2b). A further 36 field-years had a planting record from an earlier year only, 7 had no usable yield value and 2 had no harvest data. Within the 48 observations, soil clay and humus were on file for one field and dated irrigation for every season from 2018 onwards; the five field-years of 2015 to 2017 have no irrigation record and were run as rainfed. Fertiliser rates in kilograms of nutrient per hectare existed for the seasons 2023 to 2025, imported from the farm's fertiliser-plan documents, plus one manual entry in 2018; after the season they covered 17 observations in 8 field-years for nitrogen (24 to 223 kg N/ha) and 9 observations in 4 field-years for potassium (135 to 355 kg K/ha). The remaining 14 field-years had no nutrient rate on file: the planter logged fertiliser on every potato pass as a flow percentage of its nominal setting, but that quantity is not a rate and did not enter the model.

Observed yields averaged 58 t/ha with a sample standard deviation of 16.5 t/ha across the 48 observations, from 27 to 100 t/ha. Within a field-year the varieties spanned a median of 11 t/ha and at most 37 t/ha, but this within-field-year variation was 23 % of the total variance; 77 % lay between field-years.

### 3.2 Performance of the implemented plugin

Under the plugin's implemented default a nutrient with no logged application is modelled with zero applied, so on the 31 observations without a nitrogen record and the 39 without a potassium record the uncalibrated potassium floor of 0.3 capped the estimate at 30 % of potential yield whatever the weather. At field-year level the literature defaults underpredicted by 42 t/ha with a typical error of 78 % of the mean yield (Table 3). Calibration, which can move only the nitrogen parameters, could raise that cap only by inflating potential yield: every fold returned the same triple, potential yield at its upper search bound of 112.5 t/ha, nitrogen factor 2.28 and floor 0.36, and the calibrated model still underpredicted by 21 t/ha with a typical error of 49 % and an efficiency of -2.5. The model attributed the limitation to potassium in 40 of 48 observations. This is the result a user of GeoDataFarm 3.5.0 would have obtained, and it is the pre-registered evaluation.

### 3.3 Diagnostic variant and baselines

Excluding an unlogged nutrient from the Liebig combination for that field-year, the post hoc variant, left the model water-limited on observations without a nutrient record and water- and nutrient-limited on those with one. Literature defaults then underpredicted by 31 t/ha with a typical error of 58 %, because the default potential yield of 45 t/ha is below what this farm harvests in a good year. Calibrating the three crop-level parameters removed the bias and brought the typical error to 31 %, a 47 % reduction, with a smaller miss than the defaults in 19 of 22 field-years (Figure 3a, b). It did not beat the training-set farm mean, whose typical error was 27 % and which the calibrated model out-predicted in only 12 of 22 field-years; both had negative efficiencies, -0.41 and -0.09. The pre-registered criterion, a 20 % reduction against the defaults together with a lower error than the farm mean, was therefore not met in either treatment. Fitting potential yield alone gave 32 %, so the two nitrogen parameters added little (Table 3).

The training-set same-variety mean was the strongest predictor evaluated: a typical error of 22 % of the mean yield and the only positive efficiency, +0.29, lower than the farm mean in 12 of 22 field-years (Figure 3d). Refitting potential yield per variety for the eight varieties that recur in at least two training field-years brought the model to 27 % and an efficiency of -0.06, level with the farm mean and better than the same-variety mean in only 10 of 22 field-years (Figure 3c). At the level of variety observations the per-variety fit gained nothing over the crop-level fit, because it improved some varieties and worsened others (Figure S2); its fitted potential yields ran from 77 t/ha for King Edward to 117 t/ha for Belana and rested on two to eight field-years each. These are predictive results: the variety label carried more out-of-sample information than the logged weather and management variables in this dataset, but the design does not separate the cultivar from the years, fields and management under which each cultivar was grown.

Split by record, the crop-level model had a typical error of 35 % on the 8 field-years with a nitrogen record and 29 % on the 14 without, while the farm mean had 26 % and 27 %. Split by period, the model was better than the farm mean on the 5 field-years of 2015 to 2017, 11 % against 21 %, and worse on the 17 from 2018 onwards, 35 % against 29 % (Table S3). The earlier seasons are the ones with no irrigation record, so the model ran them as rainfed and matched their yields with a water deficit it cannot verify; the seasons with recorded irrigation are where it fell behind. The pre-specified sensitivity runs confirm this: without the unrecorded seasons the model stayed at 35 % against 29 % for the farm mean, with the fitted potential yield unchanged at 89.8 t/ha, and at the quality-screened level, without the three field-years above 80 t/ha, every method improved but the gap widened, 29 % for the model and 25 % for the per-variety fit against 20 % for the farm mean and 18 % for the same-variety mean, with the fitted potential yield at 86 t/ha (Table 3, Table S4). Including the three implausible field-years thus reduced the model's disadvantage against the mean baselines. Recombining the fitted relative-yield terms multiplicatively instead of by the minimum, without refitting, gave 34 % with a bias of -5 t/ha against 31 % for the minimum, so the Liebig operator is not what holds the model back (Table S4).

### 3.4 Learning curve

With a single training field-year the three-parameter model had a median typical error of 21.2 t/ha on the remaining field-years, below the literature defaults at 34.5 t/ha but above the training-set mean of that same field-year at 18.2 t/ha (Figure 4a). The curve fell slowly and not monotonically, 22.0 at two, 20.1 at three, 18.6 at eight, 16.8 at sixteen and 17.4 at twenty training field-years, and only from sixteen on did it reach the training-set farm mean, which itself sat at 17 to 18 t/ha from three field-years on. The potential-yield-only variant followed within 1 t/ha up to twelve field-years and was worse above. The per-variety fit was indistinguishable from the crop-level fit until sixteen training field-years, because a variety needs two training field-years before it gets its own potential yield, and pulled ahead only at twenty, to 15.8 t/ha; the same-variety mean likewise matched the plain training mean below eight field-years and was the best predictor from sixteen on. Fitted potential yield from one training field-year ranged from 60 to 112 t/ha across the 22 possible choices, with a median of 89.8, and its interquartile range narrowed to 82 to 97 t/ha only at sixteen or more (Figure 4b). On this farm one season fixes potential yield to that season's level, and whatever a variety contributes takes most of the record to learn.

### 3.5 Parameter stability under resampling

A parameter is well determined when refitting it on different subsets of the data gives nearly the same value; three views are given, and they measure different things. The 22 leave-one-field-year-out folds share 20 of their 21 training field-years, and the fitted values can only fall on the search grid, whose fine steps are 7.6 t/ha, 0.24 and 0.08, so their agreement is partly numerical resolution: potential yield came out at 89.8 t/ha in 21 folds and 97.4 in one, the nitrogen factor at 1.68 in 18, the floor at 0.52 in 18 and at its lower bound of 0 in one (Figure 5). The 50 bootstrap samples, each drawing 22 field-years with replacement, measure predictive stability: potential yield had 5th, 50th and 95th percentiles of 82.3, 89.8 and 97.4 t/ha and never reached a search bound; the nitrogen factor 0.48, 1.68 and 2.04, never at a bound; the floor 0, 0.52 and 0.64, at its lower bound in 8 of 50 samples. The one-dimensional profiles of the objective, the minimum of the sum of squared errors over the other axis at the all-data fit, measure practical identifiability: the sum of squares stays within 5 % of its minimum for potential yield between 85 and 93 t/ha, for the floor between 0.45 and 0.55 at the fitted factor, and for the nitrogen factor from 1.38 up to the search bound of 3.0 (Figure S1, Table S4). Potential yield is thus relatively stable under resampling and determined to roughly plus or minus 10 % by the data; the nitrogen factor is not, and the floor's apparent precision in the profile depends on the factor being held at its fitted value. Eight field-years with a fertiliser rate do not determine two nitrogen parameters.

### 3.6 Model-attributed limiting factors

The limiting factor is reported here as the modelled term with the lowest relative yield when that term is below 0.95; it is the model's attribution, not an agronomically verified limitation, and the model's own label, which reads "none" whenever no nutrient is modelled, is not used. By this definition the calibrated model attributed limitation to water in 33 of 48 observations, nitrogen in 11, potassium in 1 and to nothing in 3 (Figure 6a). The water relative yield was below 0.95 in 20 of 22 field-years, lowest at 0.46 on a 2018 field with 108 mm of logged irrigation against 136 mm of rain, then 0.51 on a 2023 field with none logged, and 0.56 to 0.60 on the four field-years of 2015 and 2016 run as rainfed for want of a record (Figure 6b). Part of the attributed water limitation is therefore an artefact of the missing pre-2018 irrigation record; the sensitivity run without those seasons shows that this does not move the fitted potential yield, but the water term cannot be validated on them. Nitrogen was attributed in all 8 observations of 2025, 6 of them on one field whose journal recorded only a 24 kg N/ha top-dressing and not the planting application, in 2 observations of 2018 with a single 60 kg N/ha entry and in 1 of 2024; those attributions reflect the record, not the crop.

### 3.7 Within-field productivity index

Figure 7 lays out which fields had a yield map in which year and which consecutive pairs could be scored. Thirty pairs of consecutive harvest years on 19 fields were on file; 21 pairs on 15 fields had enough cells with both maps to be scored, and 13 of these, on 8 fields, also had a soil layer: EM38 conductivity at one or two depths on 7 fields and laboratory sampling on one. The EM38 surveys date from 2016 to 2019 and were used unfiltered after removal of the no-data value; the laboratory layers are the 2018 sampling. Because the farm rotates potato with cereals and oilseed rape, 20 of the 21 pairs cross a crop boundary: 11 run from potato to a cereal or rape, 6 the other way, 3 from cereal to cereal and one from potato to potato; the crop labels are inferred from yield magnitude, since the cereal seasons have no planting record.

Year-to-year rank persistence. The index built from the previous season's yield map alone is a monotone transform of that map, so its rank correlation with the next season's map measures how much of the within-field ranking persists across the rotation, not any property of the index. That correlation had a median of 0.18 (interquartile range 0.08 to 0.32) over the 21 pairs, was positive in 19 and above 0.2 in 9; aggregated to one median per field it was 0.14 over 15 fields (Table 4). The top fifth of the field by previous yield out-yielded the bottom fifth by a median of 9 % of the field mean. The correlation was highest from cereal to cereal (0.28, n = 3), intermediate from potato to cereal (0.18, n = 11) and lowest from cereal to potato (0.07, n = 6). Figure 8 shows the pair at the median correlation.

Combining layers. On the 13 pairs with a soil layer the yield-only correlation was 0.15 (0.08 to 0.24). The plugin's default, one weight per layer, gave -0.03 (-0.13 to 0.11) on the same pairs and was lower than the yield-only index in 12 of the 13 (Figure 9, Table 4); one weight per layer group gave -0.01 and a z-score average -0.01, each also lower in 12 of 13, and the soil-only index -0.09. The soil layers were not without association: conductivity correlated with the next season's yield at a median of -0.15 across the 17 field-year and depth combinations, positive in 4, from -0.42 on one field to +0.33 and +0.62 on another, while the nine laboratory layers on the sampled field stayed within -0.17 to +0.17. Whether that association adds anything beyond the previous yield map is the relevant question, and the answer in this dataset is no: after the next season's yield rank was regressed on the previous season's rank, the conductivity layers correlated with the residual at a median absolute value of 0.11, above 0.2 in 3 of 17 cases, and the laboratory layers at 0.11 with a maximum of 0.15 (Table S5). The post hoc variant that weights each layer by its absolute rank correlation with the previous yield map, reversing the rank of a negatively associated layer, reached 0.15 (0.08 to 0.22), lower than yield-only in 5 of 13 pairs; it recovers approximately the yield-only level and no more, as expected of weights constructed to agree with the yield map. Soil weights of 0.5 and 2 and three or seven classes moved the default index's correlation by less than 0.03 (Table S5). The class-separation test on the spatially thinned subsample is reported in Table S5 only, since its outcome tracked the number of cells left after thinning rather than the index.

""")

