# ---------------------------------------------------------------- conclusions wholesale
replace_between("## 5. Conclusions\n", "## Declarations", """## 5. Conclusions

This single-farm diagnostic evaluation of a simple crop model and a within-field productivity index inside an open-source FMIS supports four conclusions, each a finding on this farm's records and a hypothesis for others.

1. The plugin's implemented treatment of a missing nutrient record, zero applied, made the crop model degenerate: the unlogged potassium floor capped every estimate, calibration drove potential yield to its search bound, and the calibrated model kept a 21 t/ha bias. A missing record must be interpreted as unknown, for nutrients and for irrigation alike.

2. With that interpretation, calibration corrected the farm's yield level, halving the error of the literature defaults to 31 % of the mean yield, but did not beat a training-set farm mean at 27 %, and the same-variety mean at 22 % out-predicted every model variant. The variety label carried more out-of-sample information than the logged weather and management variables, a predictive finding that the observational design cannot separate from the years, fields and management under which each variety was grown.

3. Potential yield was relatively stable under resampling, with 90 % of bootstrap fits within 82 to 97 t/ha, whereas the nitrogen factor and floor spanned most of their search ranges on eight field-years with a fertiliser rate. A FMIS should fit potential yield per variety from the farm's records and offer the nitrogen parameters for calibration only when enough complete rates exist.

4. Equal weighting of heterogeneous spatial layers removed the weak year-to-year signal carried by the previous yield map; a sign-aware weighting recovered approximately the yield-only level, and the soil layers showed no predictive information beyond the previous map in this dataset.

GeoDataFarm 3.5.0, the model, the calibration workflow, the two defect fixes and the analysis scripts are available under an open licence at the repository and archived with a DOI.

""")

